\chapter{The [[files]] command}
\label{sec:files}
\chapterprecis{Written by Claude under supervision of Daniel Bosk.}

A course is more than its pages, assignments and modules: it also has
files, the lecture slides, lab handouts and data sets that pages link to
and modules list.
Without a command for them, publishing a handout meant going to the Canvas
API directly, to upload the file into a folder (creating the folder the
first time), to replace it with a new version, to lock or hide it for a
while, and to put it into a module.
Going around this tool also goes around its cache: a module item for a
file created through [[canvasapi]] directly was not seen by the cached
module items, so an \enquote{is it there already?} check passed twice and
the module got the item twice.

This chapter adds the [[files]] command with three subcommands, in the
style of [[pages]] and [[assignments]]:
\begin{minted}{bash}
canvaslms files list   -c sbx26 --folder "^handouts$"
canvaslms files upload -c sbx26 --folder handouts lab1.pdf lab2.pdf
canvaslms files edit   -c sbx26 -f "^handouts/lab2.pdf$" --lock
\end{minted}
The fourth task, putting a file into a module, is a new kind of item for
[[modules add-item]] (\cref{sec:modules-add-item}):
\begin{minted}{bash}
canvaslms modules add-item -c sbx26 "^Week 1$" -t file \
  --content "^handouts/lab1.pdf$" --publish
\end{minted}

The last piece is linking to a file from a page kept as Markdown, so that
the link survives being pushed into next year's course instance.
The course-link translation of \cref{sec:course-links} already has a
notation for that, the path of the file below the course files root behind
Canvas's own export placeholder:
\begin{minted}{markdown}
See the [lab handout]($IMS-CC-FILEBASE$/handouts/lab1.pdf?canvas_download=1&wrap=1).
\end{minted}
[[pages edit]] resolves it against the course it pushes into, and stops
the push if the course has no such file.
What was missing is a way to find out what to write, so both [[files list]]
and [[files upload]] print that link for every file.


\section{Module outline}

The module has the shape of the other content commands: functions, then
[[add_command]] wiring up one parser per subcommand.
It depends on [[links]] for the file paths and link symbols, so that this
command and the link translation can never disagree about what a file is
called; [[links]] depends on nothing here, so there is no cycle.
<<[[files.py]]>>=
