\section{Naming a file: its path below the course files root}

A file needs a name that a user can type and a regex can match.
Its display name is not enough: two folders can each hold a
[[lab1.pdf]], and a course that keeps last year's slides in an archive
folder has many such pairs.
The id is unique but means nothing to a reader and changes when a file is
replaced (\cref{sec:files-upload}).
We use the path below the course files root, [[handouts/lab1.pdf]]: it is
unique within the course, it is what a user sees when browsing Files, and
it is how the link translation names a file (\cref{sec:course-links}), so
the name in a [[files]] command and the name in a link are the same.

Canvas lists a course's files and folders in two flat listings, each
paginated, and a file knows only the id of its folder.
Two listings and a dictionary from folder id to path therefore name every
file, where asking for each file's folder would cost one request per file.
The path of a folder comes from [[links.folder_path]], which strips the
[[course files]] prefix that every folder's full name starts with.
We keep the path, and the course for the output, on the file object.
<<functions>>=
def list_course_files(course):
  """Return the files of `course`, each with its `path` and `course` set.

  The path is the folder path below the course files root followed by
  the display name, e.g. `handouts/lab1.pdf`.
  """
  folders = {folder.id: canvaslms.cli.links.folder_path(folder)
             for folder in course.get_folders()}
  files = []
  for file in course.get_files():
    file = canvaslms.cli.utils.ensure_file_attrs(file)
    file.course = course
    file.path = join_path(folders.get(file.folder_id, ""),
                          file.display_name)
    files.append(file)
  return files

def join_path(folder, name):
  """Return the path of `name` in the folder at path `folder`."""
  return f"{folder}/{name}" if folder else name
@

A file is selected by a regex on its path or its id, the way modules and
pages are selected by name or id.
Matching the path rather than the display name lets one regex select a
whole folder ([[^handouts/]]) as well as one file in it.
The [[--folder]] regex narrows by folder alone, which matters when the
folder name also occurs in file names; both must match when both are given.
<<functions>>=
def filter_files(course, regex=".*", folder_regex=None):
  """Return the files of `course` whose path or id matches `regex`.

  With `folder_regex`, the file's folder path must match it too.
  """
  pattern = re.compile(regex)
  folder_pattern = re.compile(folder_regex) if folder_regex else None
  result = []
  for file in list_course_files(course):
    if not (pattern.search(file.path) or pattern.search(str(file.id))):
      continue
    folder = file.path.rpartition("/")[0]
    if folder_pattern and not folder_pattern.search(folder):
      continue
    result.append(file)
  return result
@

Let's verify that every file gets its path, root files included, and that
the two regexes select as described.
