\section{The link to paste into a page}
\label{sec:files-link}

Every file the command prints comes with the link a Markdown page should
use for it.
The link is built by [[links.file_symbol]], the same function that turns a
file link into a symbol when a page is pulled out of Canvas, so it is
exactly what [[pages edit]] resolves on the way back in.
It names the file by path, not id, and so it stays valid when the file is
replaced and when the page is pushed into another course that has the same
file at the same path.
We append [[?canvas_download=1&wrap=1]], the form Canvas's own editor
writes for a link to a file: it resolves to [[/files/<id>/download?wrap=1]],
which shows the file wrapped in the course navigation.

A row has the course, the path, the id, the two flags [[files edit]]
changes, and the link.
The id is there for scripts that need the numeric form; the flags are
spelled as words, as [[pages list]] spells [[published]] and
[[unpublished]], so that the output reads without a legend.
<<constants>>=
LINK_QUERY = "?canvas_download=1&wrap=1"
<<functions>>=
def file_link(file):
  """Return the symbolic link a page should use for `file`."""
  folder, _, name = file.path.rpartition("/")
  return canvaslms.cli.links.file_symbol(folder, name) + LINK_QUERY

def file_row(file):
  """Return the output row for `file`."""
  return [file.course.course_code, file.path, file.id,
          "locked" if file.locked else "unlocked",
          "hidden" if file.hidden else "visible",
          file_link(file)]
@

The promise of this section is that what we print resolves to the file it
was printed for.
Let's verify it with the link translation itself: the links of a file in
a plain folder, in a folder with a space, in a folder with a [[#]] and at
the root are resolved against the fake course.
The fake answers the folder-path lookup that resolution makes only at the
path Canvas understands, with the folder names percent-encoded, which is
what the lookup must send (\cref{sec:course-links}).
<<test functions>>=
@responses.activate
def test_file_link_resolves_to_the_file():
  serve_listing(responses)
  course = canvasapi_course(Canvas(SERVER, "token"))
  for file in filter_files(course, "lab1|intro|^14$|syllabus"):
    folder = file.path.rpartition("/")[0]
    chain = [f for f in FOLDERS if f["full_name"] == "course files"] \
      + [f for f in FOLDERS if f["full_name"] == f"course files/{folder}"]
    by_path = f"{API}/courses/1/folders/by_path"
    responses.get(f"{by_path}/{urllib.parse.quote(folder, safe='/')}"
                  if folder else by_path, json=chain)
    responses.get(f"{API}/folders/{chain[-1]['id']}/files",
                  json=[f for f in FILES if f["folder_id"] == chain[-1]["id"]])
    html = f'<a href="{file_link(file)}">x</a>'
    resolved, problems = canvaslms.cli.links.resolve_links(html, course)
    assert problems == []
    assert f'href="/courses/1/files/{file.id}/download?wrap=1"' in resolved

def test_file_link_encodes_the_path():
  file = argparse.Namespace(path="old slides/intro a.pdf")
  assert file_link(file) == ("$IMS-CC-FILEBASE$/old%20slides/intro%20a.pdf"
                             "?canvas_download=1&wrap=1")
  file = argparse.Namespace(path="syllabus.pdf")
  assert file_link(file).startswith("$IMS-CC-FILEBASE$/syllabus.pdf?")
