\section{The [[files upload]] subcommand}
\label{sec:files-upload}

[[files upload]] puts local files into a folder of the course, given as a
path below the course files root:
\begin{minted}{bash}
canvaslms files upload -c sbx26 --folder handouts/week1 lab1.pdf lab2.pdf
\end{minted}
Canvas creates the folder, and any folder above it, when it does not
exist, so the first upload into a folder needs no separate step.
The folder is required: without one, Canvas puts a course upload into a
folder called [[unfiled]], which is rarely what anyone wants; [[/]] names
the root itself.

\subsection{Replacing a file}

Uploading a file whose name is taken in the folder replaces the old file
by default, which is also Canvas's default.
Replacing is what a course needs when a handout is corrected: in the
sandbox course (2026-10-29), the replacement got a new id, yet a module
item pointing at the old file pointed at the new one afterwards, the
replacement kept the old file's locked and hidden flags, and the old file
disappeared from the course's file listing.
Links in pages moved too: a page pushed with the file's symbolic link,
which resolved to the old id, linked to the new id after the replacement.
So replacing breaks no reference and changes nothing the students see
except the content.
[[--rename]] keeps the old file instead, and Canvas gives the new one a
free name ([[lab1-1.pdf]]).

\subsection{Locking and hiding on upload}

A new file is visible to students as soon as it is uploaded, the way
Canvas's own upload works.
This differs from [[modules add-item]], whose new items are unpublished
(\cref{sec:modules-add-item}), and the difference is deliberate: a module
item is a step in the students' path that the author should read in place
first, while a file is usually uploaded to be linked from somewhere, and a
locked file behind a published link is a broken link to the students.
[[--lock]] and [[--hidden]] set the flags right after the upload, since
Canvas's upload request does not take them; a replaced file keeps its
flags anyway, so they matter for new files.

Every uploaded file is printed as [[files list]] prints it, with the link
to use in a page.
<<add files upload subcommand>>=
